This machine's own chains and sets (PengyOS). Empty unless this box needs them.

/etc/nftables.conf includes every *.nft file here at table level, inside
`table inet pengyos`. A file holds whole `chain` and `set` definitions, which
is what a router's NAT needs:

    set router_ssh_ratelimit { type ipv4_addr; flags dynamic,timeout; timeout 1m; }
    chain router_dnat {
        type nat hook prerouting priority dstnat; policy accept;
        iifname "wan0" tcp dport 22 dnat ip to 10.0.23.3:22
    }
    chain router_snat {
        type nat hook postrouting priority srcnat; policy accept;
        oifname "wan0" meta nfproto ipv4 masquerade
    }

This is an inet table, so NAT statements name the family: `dnat ip to`,
`snat ip to`. Sets live here, never inside a chain. A chain hooked to input at
a priority below 0 runs before the image's input chain, and a drop there is
final; that is how to keep the image's always-open ssh off a WAN link:

    chain router_wan_guard {
        type filter hook input priority -10; policy accept;
        iifname "wan0" tcp dport 22 drop
    }

Prefix every chain and set with router_ (or this machine's own word) so it
never collides with a name the image uses. Check, then reload:

    sudo nft -c -f /etc/nftables.conf && sudo systemctl reload nftables
